1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,059
Each human cell holds about two metres of DNA, in a nucleus just ten to twenty microns across.

2
00:00:06,733 --> 00:00:11,533
So how does that thread pack into chromosomes? In two moves: spools, then loops.

3
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:17,056
Up close, the thread is a double helix, two nanometres wide.

4
00:00:17,733 --> 00:00:24,859
It wraps almost twice around a spool of eight histone proteins. Spool and DNA together make a nucleosome.

5
00:00:25,533 --> 00:00:30,995
Repeated along the strand like beads on a string, nucleosomes make it about seven times shorter.

6
00:00:32,667 --> 00:00:34,373
But that is still far too long.

7
00:00:35,633 --> 00:00:41,969
Before a cell divides, protein rings called condensin pull the string into loops along a central axis.

8
00:00:42,667 --> 00:00:47,445
Stacked loops thicken the axis into a chromosome: two copies, joined in the middle.

9
00:00:53,033 --> 00:00:57,129
Spools, then loops: about ten thousand times shorter than the bare thread.

10
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:02,533
To read more, start with Nature Scitable on DNA packaging.
